WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.919
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.919 --> 00:00:05.380
My father who art in heaven--

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:09.426
my dad got hurt by the police.

00:00:09.426 --> 00:00:14.097
Please tell him to
get a better job.

00:00:14.097 --> 00:00:16.057
In Jesus name, we say amen.

00:00:16.057 --> 00:00:16.433
Amen.

00:00:16.433 --> 00:00:25.650
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:28.194
Kenya has been ranked
dismally as one

00:00:28.194 --> 00:00:30.488
of the most corrupt
countries in the world.

00:00:30.488 --> 00:00:30.989
MP!

00:00:30.989 --> 00:00:31.239
MP!.

00:00:31.239 --> 00:00:31.531
MP!

00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:31.865
MP!

00:00:31.865 --> 00:00:32.282
MP.

00:00:34.993 --> 00:00:37.162
We have a right-- we
have a right to protest.

00:00:37.162 --> 00:00:38.538
Boniface Mwangi.

00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:42.584
Folks, this young man
is, one word, fearless.

00:00:46.755 --> 00:00:48.965
Why are you arresting me?

00:00:48.965 --> 00:00:50.383
Why are-- what have I done?

00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:51.718
What have I done?

00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:55.221
Where are you going?

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:57.557
I'm going to topple
the government.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:00:59.225
Whoa.

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:01.227
I can only imagine
how scared I'd be,

00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:02.896
seeing my husband
taking to the streets.

00:01:02.896 --> 00:01:05.774
Now he wants to try
his hand at politics.

00:01:05.982 --> 00:01:06.775
What?

00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:08.902
My heart is made up
that I'm going to vie.

00:01:08.902 --> 00:01:11.488
Ha, ha, hah Whoa.

00:01:11.696 --> 00:01:12.989
This is how you're
telling it to me.

00:01:12.989 --> 00:01:13.490
I mean.

00:01:17.869 --> 00:01:20.497
He's a good man. We need to vote for him.

00:01:24.292 --> 00:01:27.128
Does Boniface Mwangi
have deep pockets?

00:01:27.128 --> 00:01:29.798
No, he doesn't is
he on the right side

00:01:29.798 --> 00:01:31.508
of the tribal kingpin?

00:01:31.508 --> 00:01:32.509
No, he's not.

00:01:32.509 --> 00:01:35.887
He's going to be a casualty
of other forces beyond him.

00:01:37.097 --> 00:01:38.431
Do not come home.

00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:39.808
They will kill you.

00:01:39.808 --> 00:01:42.727
Activist Boniface Mwangi
receiving death threats

00:01:42.727 --> 00:01:45.814
that, for the first time, target
his wife and children as well.

00:01:45.814 --> 00:01:49.651
It feels like just jumping
into a river of crocodiles.

00:01:49.651 --> 00:01:59.619
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:10.338 --> 00:02:11.214
Hi, everyone.

00:02:11.214 --> 00:02:12.882
My name is Roger Ross Williams.

00:02:12.882 --> 00:02:17.095
And welcome to the Academy
Visiting Artist Series.

00:02:17.095 --> 00:02:19.389
I'm a documentary
filmmaker based in New York

00:02:19.389 --> 00:02:20.974
and Amsterdam, the Netherlands.

00:02:20.974 --> 00:02:22.600
And I've been
making documentaries

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:25.019
to show real life
and real situations

00:02:25.019 --> 00:02:27.272
and real people for a long time.

00:02:27.272 --> 00:02:29.899
A number of my films
are focused in Africa.

00:02:29.899 --> 00:02:32.777
And I'm passionate about
helping the next generation

00:02:32.777 --> 00:02:36.030
of African filmmakers
tell their own stories.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:38.449
I am so excited to be
here in conversation

00:02:38.449 --> 00:02:40.994
with the amazing African
filmmaker and dear

00:02:40.994 --> 00:02:43.788
friend of mine, Toni Kamau.

00:02:43.788 --> 00:02:48.168
Toni is an IDA, PDA
nominee and the youngest

00:02:48.168 --> 00:02:50.545
female African
documentary producer

00:02:50.545 --> 00:02:53.548
to be invited as a member
of the Academy of Motion

00:02:53.548 --> 00:02:56.801
Pictures, Arts, and
Sciences, documentary branch.

00:02:56.801 --> 00:03:00.013
As a producer and
writer and founder of We

00:03:00.013 --> 00:03:02.807
Are Not the Machine, a
Nairobi-based production

00:03:02.807 --> 00:03:06.102
company, she tells stories
of outsiders, rebels,

00:03:06.102 --> 00:03:08.021
and changemakers.

00:03:08.021 --> 00:03:12.442
The Sundance Special Jury prize
winner and POV co-production

00:03:12.442 --> 00:03:16.738
Softie produced by Toni and
directed by Sam Soko premiered

00:03:16.738 --> 00:03:18.740
at the Sundance in 20--

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:22.994
Sundace Festival in 2020 in
the world cinema documentary

00:03:22.994 --> 00:03:24.746
feature competition.

00:03:24.746 --> 00:03:27.916
It was selected as the
opening night film of Hot Docs

00:03:27.916 --> 00:03:32.212
and won the Oscar-qualifying
best African documentary award

00:03:32.212 --> 00:03:34.339
at the Durban Film Festival.

00:03:34.339 --> 00:03:38.009
Toni is currently in production
on a feature documentary

00:03:38.009 --> 00:03:40.428
on home and belonging
and in development

00:03:40.428 --> 00:03:43.431
on a documentary series
exploring the legacy

00:03:43.431 --> 00:03:45.642
of colonialism in Kenya.

00:03:45.642 --> 00:03:50.063
I am so excited to get a
chance to talk to Toni.

00:03:50.063 --> 00:03:54.067
And Toni, thank
you for being here.

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:57.320
Well, thank you so much for
the introduction, Roger.

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:57.820
It's--

00:03:57.820 --> 00:03:58.696
Yes.

00:03:58.696 --> 00:04:00.990
Good evening, talking
to you- it feels

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:05.328
like this is coming full
circle because you are actually

00:04:05.328 --> 00:04:10.917
the first documentary
director I ever saw

00:04:10.917 --> 00:04:13.670
at IDFA who looked like me.

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:15.588
It was God Loves Uganda.

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:17.840
And I keep on telling
everyone the story.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:21.427
Pete, a Kenyan
director, and I, we

00:04:21.427 --> 00:04:23.805
saw you for the first time
talking about your experience.

00:04:23.805 --> 00:04:25.848
And I think at that
moment, we realized

00:04:25.848 --> 00:04:29.060
that we, as people
of color, could tell

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:31.813
stories for the global stage.

00:04:31.813 --> 00:04:34.816
So it's just amazing to be
here and talking to you.

00:04:34.816 --> 00:04:38.486
Well, I was really excited when
I met you for the first time.

00:04:38.486 --> 00:04:42.115
Because for so long, so
many people have been--

00:04:42.115 --> 00:04:43.908
from the outside, have
been going to Africa

00:04:43.908 --> 00:04:45.410
and telling your stories.

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:49.080
And it was so exciting to meet
the community of filmmakers who

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:51.416
are telling your own stories.

00:04:51.416 --> 00:04:54.460
It's about time.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:57.839
I think I'm going to start
with the beginning for you

00:04:57.839 --> 00:05:02.051
and your career and sort of,
what inspired you to pursue

00:05:02.051 --> 00:05:05.596
a film and a career in
filmmaking and documentary

00:05:05.596 --> 00:05:07.307
filmmaking.

00:05:07.307 --> 00:05:07.807
Yeah.

00:05:07.807 --> 00:05:12.061
I mean, I've always loved story.

00:05:12.061 --> 00:05:15.565
When I was a child,
my mother was away.

00:05:15.565 --> 00:05:18.526
My late mother was away
for most of my childhood.

00:05:18.526 --> 00:05:21.904
She was studying
in the UK, her PhD.

00:05:21.904 --> 00:05:25.908
And the only way I was really
able to understand the world

00:05:25.908 --> 00:05:28.828
that she lived in, which just
seemed so separate and alien,

00:05:28.828 --> 00:05:30.371
was by watching TV.

00:05:30.371 --> 00:05:33.124
So I think for me, TV
holds a special place

00:05:33.124 --> 00:05:36.627
because it gives you a chance
to explore other worlds

00:05:36.627 --> 00:05:38.046
and other cultures.

00:05:38.046 --> 00:05:39.047
And yeah.

00:05:39.047 --> 00:05:41.591
I think I went to
film school in Kenya.

00:05:41.591 --> 00:05:44.260
I was initially interested
in working in fiction.

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:47.972
But I watched Hoop
Dreams, absolutely

00:05:47.972 --> 00:05:50.141
loved the documentary.

00:05:50.141 --> 00:05:52.852
And it was just so interesting
to understand that, oh,

00:05:52.852 --> 00:05:56.689
my God, you can get this insight
into people's experiences.

00:05:56.689 --> 00:06:00.151
And you can find this
sense of universality

00:06:00.151 --> 00:06:03.780
that I felt was kind of
missing from fiction in a way

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:07.200
that I can't really
articulate right now.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:12.789
But it just felt like it was
something so special to me.

00:06:12.789 --> 00:06:14.999
Yeah.

00:06:14.999 --> 00:06:19.295
You know, I think it's
important for our audience

00:06:19.295 --> 00:06:25.927
to understand the difference
between documentary and fiction

00:06:25.927 --> 00:06:26.886
storytelling.

00:06:26.886 --> 00:06:31.474
And the beautiful thing about
documentary and something

00:06:31.474 --> 00:06:34.394
like Hoop Dreams is
that you can live,

00:06:34.394 --> 00:06:40.775
you can follow a real-life
story for, sometimes,

00:06:40.775 --> 00:06:43.861
people, 10 years or
longer, or 20 years,

00:06:43.861 --> 00:06:48.783
and really get the full
scope of the human existence.

00:06:48.783 --> 00:06:50.660
And that's a beautiful thing.

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:55.373
So tell me, you know, what kind
of stories do you like to tell?

00:06:55.373 --> 00:06:59.377
What is the sort of, you
know, what does Tony love?

00:06:59.377 --> 00:07:01.796
What are-- you're
like, you know,

00:07:01.796 --> 00:07:07.510
you tend to really focus
on stories of people

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:11.681
like Softie, like
challenging the system.

00:07:11.681 --> 00:07:14.892
But there's also a deep
humanity to the types of stories

00:07:14.892 --> 00:07:15.518
that you tell.

00:07:15.518 --> 00:07:19.564
But tell us what you
love, what kind of stories

00:07:19.564 --> 00:07:20.648
you really love.

00:07:20.648 --> 00:07:21.691
Yeah.

00:07:21.691 --> 00:07:25.403
So really drawn to working
with talented directors

00:07:25.403 --> 00:07:30.491
like Sam Soko of Softie
and Pete of I am Samuel.

00:07:30.491 --> 00:07:34.120
And you know, we
come from a society--

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:35.997
I'll speak specifically
about Kenya

00:07:35.997 --> 00:07:37.915
because that's where I grew up.

00:07:37.915 --> 00:07:42.545
We come from a country where
from the time you go to school,

00:07:42.545 --> 00:07:45.131
actually, kindergarten,
you're told to conform.

00:07:45.131 --> 00:07:46.966
You're told to toe the line.

00:07:46.966 --> 00:07:50.428
You're never allowed
to question authority.

00:07:50.428 --> 00:07:52.472
Everything is supposed
to be cookie cutter.

00:07:52.472 --> 00:07:54.140
And it's because
our education is

00:07:54.140 --> 00:08:01.689
based on colonial ways of
framing the world and training

00:08:01.689 --> 00:08:05.401
people to be these factory
workers or people work on farms

00:08:05.401 --> 00:08:06.944
for the colonial masters.

00:08:06.944 --> 00:08:13.034
So people like Boniface,
our subjects in Softie,

00:08:13.034 --> 00:08:15.703
are very interesting to me
because I always wonder,

00:08:15.703 --> 00:08:18.706
what is it about
them that makes them

00:08:18.706 --> 00:08:20.958
rail against the system,
that makes them actually

00:08:20.958 --> 00:08:25.838
question their place in society,
or how society's working.

00:08:25.838 --> 00:08:28.799
So I find those kind of
people very, very fascinating,

00:08:28.799 --> 00:08:31.886
specifically because of
the community I grew up in.

00:08:31.886 --> 00:08:33.930
And I want to be a little
bit more like them.

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:37.683
So I'm really attracted to those
kind of stories, to be honest.

00:08:37.683 --> 00:08:39.560
Because I'm just
constantly trying

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:44.607
to unravel and unpack that with
every single project that I get

00:08:44.607 --> 00:08:46.192
attached to.

00:08:46.192 --> 00:08:46.734
Yeah.

00:08:46.734 --> 00:08:52.073
Yeah, And taking on
a project like Softie

00:08:52.073 --> 00:08:57.995
was really interesting because
you had all of this footage.

00:08:57.995 --> 00:09:03.167
And talk about
like, as a producer,

00:09:03.167 --> 00:09:06.963
as a filmmaker, how you
found that unique story

00:09:06.963 --> 00:09:10.091
within the footage.

00:09:10.091 --> 00:09:10.675
Wow.

00:09:10.675 --> 00:09:11.259
Yes.

00:09:11.259 --> 00:09:14.011
That was, outside
of fundraising,

00:09:14.011 --> 00:09:15.680
that was one of the
biggest, I think,

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:18.683
challenges that
we had in Softie.

00:09:18.683 --> 00:09:22.353
Because Soko, the director,
and the team, I mean,

00:09:22.353 --> 00:09:24.272
we'd filmed over 500 hours.

00:09:24.272 --> 00:09:28.109
We had over 500
hours of footage.

00:09:28.109 --> 00:09:36.117
And it was very easy to, maybe,
try and tell this narrative

00:09:36.117 --> 00:09:39.370
about a country that's
struggling with democracy

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:41.914
and this activist who's
always on the streets.

00:09:41.914 --> 00:09:45.042
But Soko, as a director
and us as a film team,

00:09:45.042 --> 00:09:46.919
we're very committed
to telling the family

00:09:46.919 --> 00:09:48.588
story, the personal story.

00:09:48.588 --> 00:09:51.841
Because we felt that was
also important to look

00:09:51.841 --> 00:09:55.970
at this young marriage,
two people struggling

00:09:55.970 --> 00:09:57.096
to balance priorities.

00:09:57.096 --> 00:09:58.764
Because we felt
there was a parallel

00:09:58.764 --> 00:10:03.811
between that and citizens
of a country balancing

00:10:03.811 --> 00:10:06.480
their relationship, or trying
to navigate their relationship,

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:08.941
with a young state.

00:10:08.941 --> 00:10:13.654
So I think for us, we really
had a lot of conversations

00:10:13.654 --> 00:10:18.242
as a film team, just
about like, what's

00:10:18.242 --> 00:10:21.621
the core of the story, what do
we want audiences to leave here

00:10:21.621 --> 00:10:22.163
with.

00:10:22.163 --> 00:10:24.999
And when we felt that we
had an understanding of what

00:10:24.999 --> 00:10:28.419
our arc would be,
it was, I think,

00:10:28.419 --> 00:10:30.963
easier for Soko
and the edit team

00:10:30.963 --> 00:10:33.758
to really be able to now
dive back into the footage

00:10:33.758 --> 00:10:36.135
and start marking footage.

00:10:36.135 --> 00:10:39.513
And once everyone
like, I think there's

00:10:39.513 --> 00:10:41.932
a bit of a stalemate where
people were talking about which

00:10:41.932 --> 00:10:45.895
scenes should be in the film,
one of our amazing editors,

00:10:45.895 --> 00:10:50.066
Ryan Mullins, just came and
just quickly edited together

00:10:50.066 --> 00:10:51.150
a rough structure.

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:53.319
And then we had
a framework to be

00:10:53.319 --> 00:10:58.658
able to kind of build around.

00:10:58.658 --> 00:11:03.120
So I would say the
beginning was fast and very

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:04.372
slow at the same time.

00:11:04.372 --> 00:11:05.289
Because it took us--

00:11:05.289 --> 00:11:08.626
it took us about a year
to get to a structure

00:11:08.626 --> 00:11:12.505
from when we actually
committed to editing.

00:11:12.505 --> 00:11:15.966
| it also happened very
fast because the edits

00:11:15.966 --> 00:11:20.096
on the computer was done in
like, a few weeks after months

00:11:20.096 --> 00:11:22.973
of talking and months of
looking through footage.

00:11:22.973 --> 00:11:24.141
Yeah.

00:11:24.141 --> 00:11:24.809
Well, yeah.

00:11:24.809 --> 00:11:27.728
I mean these films are built
in the edit room, for sure.

00:11:27.728 --> 00:11:37.279
So talk to me about the role of
a producer in documentary film,

00:11:37.279 --> 00:11:41.784
sort of, how that role
progresses and changes

00:11:41.784 --> 00:11:45.705
from development to production
to post-production to selling

00:11:45.705 --> 00:11:48.290
and marketing the film.

00:11:48.290 --> 00:11:50.543
Yeah.

00:11:50.543 --> 00:11:54.672
I think being a producer,
it's always one of, I think,

00:11:54.672 --> 00:11:57.174
the hardest roles to explain.

00:11:57.174 --> 00:11:59.927
I think, especially,
specifically in documentary

00:11:59.927 --> 00:12:02.513
because it's become so nebulous.

00:12:02.513 --> 00:12:04.181
Sometimes, people
think a producer

00:12:04.181 --> 00:12:06.892
is someone who brings money
to the table or someone who

00:12:06.892 --> 00:12:08.436
brings access.

00:12:08.436 --> 00:12:12.314
So it is something that the
industry is grappling with,

00:12:12.314 --> 00:12:14.024
just in terms of
who should actually

00:12:14.024 --> 00:12:18.612
get credited as a producer.

00:12:18.612 --> 00:12:22.575
But I would-- how
I see producing,

00:12:22.575 --> 00:12:28.831
I would say that you're the one
who ensures that the director's

00:12:28.831 --> 00:12:32.251
creative vision is protected.

00:12:32.251 --> 00:12:34.837
And you're the one who
ensures that you're

00:12:34.837 --> 00:12:37.715
able to gather
resources, whether it's

00:12:37.715 --> 00:12:40.259
a team, whether it's
fundraising, you know,

00:12:40.259 --> 00:12:42.887
whether it's the
right publicist when

00:12:42.887 --> 00:12:44.138
you get to releasing the film.

00:12:44.138 --> 00:12:47.141
So you make sure you gather
the resources that are also

00:12:47.141 --> 00:12:50.227
able to ensure that the
director's creative vision is

00:12:50.227 --> 00:12:51.103
achieved.

00:12:51.103 --> 00:12:54.398
And you're also a
therapist sometimes.

00:12:54.398 --> 00:12:54.899
Because--

00:12:54.899 --> 00:12:55.858
That's so true.

00:12:55.858 --> 00:12:59.320
--you know, directing
documentaries is not easy.

00:12:59.320 --> 00:13:03.783
So I think that's why it's
also important to just

00:13:03.783 --> 00:13:05.534
have a good relationship
with the director

00:13:05.534 --> 00:13:09.079
because you talk a lot about
just the process of making

00:13:09.079 --> 00:13:12.708
the film or the process
of dealing with characters

00:13:12.708 --> 00:13:13.417
and subjects.

00:13:13.417 --> 00:13:18.380
Because it's-- I mean, it's
a complicated relationship,

00:13:18.380 --> 00:13:20.716
the relationship between a
director and the subject.

00:13:20.716 --> 00:13:24.637
You are-- you're friends
to a certain degree

00:13:24.637 --> 00:13:28.724
but on the other hand, you
have to separate yourself

00:13:28.724 --> 00:13:32.770
from the person so you can
maintain a level of objectivity

00:13:32.770 --> 00:13:35.314
when you're trying to figure
out what the story is.

00:13:35.314 --> 00:13:36.565
So it's a delicate balance.

00:13:36.565 --> 00:13:40.069
And I think having
a producer involved,

00:13:40.069 --> 00:13:42.530
especially from the beginning,
is really important,

00:13:42.530 --> 00:13:47.076
I would say, for directors,
for documentary directors,

00:13:47.076 --> 00:13:50.079
to just have that creative
sounding-- creative sounding

00:13:50.079 --> 00:13:51.247
board.

00:13:51.247 --> 00:13:55.543
But if I was to just talk about
it in a very structured manner,

00:13:55.543 --> 00:14:00.548
I would say the producer
is, in documentary,

00:14:00.548 --> 00:14:05.427
you make sure that you're able
to get access to characters

00:14:05.427 --> 00:14:08.138
in writing, releases, so
making sure you secure releases

00:14:08.138 --> 00:14:11.934
in the beginning of
development, or at least,

00:14:11.934 --> 00:14:15.020
at some point in production,
so that you can actually

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:18.232
be able to finish the
film and release it.

00:14:18.232 --> 00:14:20.317
Then I would say it's
also about helping

00:14:20.317 --> 00:14:23.529
to put the proposal together
with the director and the pitch

00:14:23.529 --> 00:14:28.826
package, reviewing
materials, fundraising.

00:14:28.826 --> 00:14:31.537
For example, Softie,
after we fundraised we

00:14:31.537 --> 00:14:33.873
were a global team
that was spread

00:14:33.873 --> 00:14:36.750
in Kenya, Canada, the UK.

00:14:36.750 --> 00:14:40.504
So a lot of my job, when we were
getting into post-production,

00:14:40.504 --> 00:14:42.840
was just coordinating
the team and managing

00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:44.425
different interests.

00:14:44.425 --> 00:14:46.677
We all believed in the film.

00:14:46.677 --> 00:14:50.806
But we always had
to just like, keep

00:14:50.806 --> 00:14:53.642
on checking in and making sure
that we were on the same page

00:14:53.642 --> 00:14:55.352
and that we were--

00:14:55.352 --> 00:14:57.730
every time the edit
changed, we just

00:14:57.730 --> 00:14:59.857
had to make sure that we
were on the same path.

00:14:59.857 --> 00:15:04.987
And we also had to make
sure that we were all

00:15:04.987 --> 00:15:08.407
being supportive of the director
because it's very easy to lose

00:15:08.407 --> 00:15:10.409
track and for
people to be making

00:15:10.409 --> 00:15:13.120
different films within
the same project,

00:15:13.120 --> 00:15:14.496
as I have come to learn.

00:15:14.496 --> 00:15:16.415
Yeah.

00:15:16.415 --> 00:15:16.916
Yeah.

00:15:16.916 --> 00:15:20.961
And I think outside of
that, coordinating the team.

00:15:20.961 --> 00:15:23.923
It's also just about
meeting deadlines

00:15:23.923 --> 00:15:27.635
whether it's submitting for
grants, whether it's submitting

00:15:27.635 --> 00:15:29.261
the film to festivals.

00:15:29.261 --> 00:15:32.222
And once the
documentary is complete,

00:15:32.222 --> 00:15:37.478
and you've secured all releases
for music and any archive

00:15:37.478 --> 00:15:40.564
and that you made
sure that you've

00:15:40.564 --> 00:15:42.024
produced the project
within budget,

00:15:42.024 --> 00:15:44.818
you know, the next
stage is then managing

00:15:44.818 --> 00:15:48.447
the release of the film, which
was something very new for me

00:15:48.447 --> 00:15:51.116
with my first two features,
Softie and I am Samuel.

00:15:51.116 --> 00:15:55.454
Because my previous experience
was working as a documentary

00:15:55.454 --> 00:15:58.040
director and producer for
Al Jazeera, BBC World.

00:15:58.040 --> 00:16:00.668
And when you finished the
documentary, that was it.

00:16:00.668 --> 00:16:02.628
You didn't have to think
about anything else.

00:16:02.628 --> 00:16:05.005
You locked picture,
you delivered.

00:16:05.005 --> 00:16:06.423
And that was it.

00:16:06.423 --> 00:16:09.093
So I would say one of the
steepest learning curves,

00:16:09.093 --> 00:16:13.055
specifically for Softie, for
Soko and I, because we were

00:16:13.055 --> 00:16:15.683
both producers in that,
was managing the release

00:16:15.683 --> 00:16:18.727
and distribution and
dealing with sales agents.

00:16:18.727 --> 00:16:21.230
That was quite hectic.

00:16:21.230 --> 00:16:23.649
And then an awards
campaign because we

00:16:23.649 --> 00:16:25.234
got nominated to the PGA.

00:16:25.234 --> 00:16:28.988
That was quite-- that was
a steep learning curve.

00:16:28.988 --> 00:16:32.449
A whole new set of skill
sets were developed.

00:16:32.449 --> 00:16:34.326
It's a lot.

00:16:34.326 --> 00:16:36.203
And you know,
people often ask me.

00:16:36.203 --> 00:16:37.788
They're like, what
does a producer do?

00:16:37.788 --> 00:16:39.540
I'm like, they do a lot.

00:16:39.540 --> 00:16:40.749
They do everything.

00:16:40.749 --> 00:16:41.959
They're running the show.

00:16:41.959 --> 00:16:44.962
They're keeping it all
together for all of us.

00:16:44.962 --> 00:16:46.755
Let's break it down a
little bit for people

00:16:46.755 --> 00:16:49.758
who want to get into
this, into filmmaking.

00:16:49.758 --> 00:16:54.888
So the most important thing
is how do people fund?

00:16:54.888 --> 00:16:57.641
You know, how do people
fund these projects?

00:16:57.641 --> 00:16:58.851
How do you find funding?

00:16:58.851 --> 00:17:02.896
How did you go about that
with Softie and I am Samuel?

00:17:02.896 --> 00:17:04.982
Yeah.

00:17:04.982 --> 00:17:06.900
So I mean, it's interesting.

00:17:06.900 --> 00:17:09.028
In Kenya, there are
not that many sources

00:17:09.028 --> 00:17:14.658
of public financing or public
funding for factual documentary

00:17:14.658 --> 00:17:15.784
projects.

00:17:15.784 --> 00:17:20.414
A lot of times, when we're
working on this social justice

00:17:20.414 --> 00:17:23.250
documentaries, we're doing
the exact kind of work

00:17:23.250 --> 00:17:25.044
that government
public funds do not

00:17:25.044 --> 00:17:28.756
want to be released
out into the world.

00:17:28.756 --> 00:17:35.637
So I mean, how we got our
funding was first to DocuBox,

00:17:35.637 --> 00:17:37.389
which you're on the board of.

00:17:37.389 --> 00:17:38.932
I am on the board.

00:17:38.932 --> 00:17:42.895
Which was started by fellow
Academy member, Judy Kibenge,

00:17:42.895 --> 00:17:44.938
co-founded, yeah,
founded by Judy Kibenge.

00:17:44.938 --> 00:17:48.484
So we got some funding from her.

00:17:48.484 --> 00:17:51.445
And with Softie,
we also got funding

00:17:51.445 --> 00:17:53.072
from the Ford Foundation.

00:17:53.072 --> 00:17:56.241
Actually, Soko got seed funding,
before I joined the project,

00:17:56.241 --> 00:17:57.534
from Ford Foundation.

00:17:57.534 --> 00:17:58.994
Because in the
beginning, he wanted

00:17:58.994 --> 00:18:01.163
to do an activistic--
activist manual.

00:18:01.163 --> 00:18:04.124
He had no intentions of
actually doing a feature.

00:18:04.124 --> 00:18:05.626
Like, yeah, he keeps
on saying that.

00:18:05.626 --> 00:18:09.630
And I'm like, 500 hours
for an activist manual,

00:18:09.630 --> 00:18:11.381
not very efficient.

00:18:11.381 --> 00:18:13.467
No.

00:18:13.467 --> 00:18:13.967
Yeah.

00:18:13.967 --> 00:18:15.385
But it was amazing
because he keeps

00:18:15.385 --> 00:18:16.470
on the telling that story.

00:18:16.470 --> 00:18:18.847
And I just think it's the most
amazing story in the world

00:18:18.847 --> 00:18:20.641
because it's such
a fantastic film.

00:18:20.641 --> 00:18:21.141
Yeah.

00:18:21.141 --> 00:18:21.934
So yeah.

00:18:21.934 --> 00:18:25.896
So he got seed funding from
Ford Foundation around 2013

00:18:25.896 --> 00:18:30.317
and Doc Society and
Hot Docs came on board.

00:18:30.317 --> 00:18:33.320
I joined the
project after he had

00:18:33.320 --> 00:18:35.864
done about four years,
done the majority

00:18:35.864 --> 00:18:37.991
of principal photography.

00:18:37.991 --> 00:18:41.495
And then we got
into Hot Docs forum.

00:18:41.495 --> 00:18:45.415
And we were the
first Kenyan team

00:18:45.415 --> 00:18:49.837
to pitch at Hot Docs forum,
which we had no idea what

00:18:49.837 --> 00:18:50.921
we were going to do there.

00:18:50.921 --> 00:18:53.215
But we went there anyway.

00:18:53.215 --> 00:18:54.967
And at first, we
were really nervous

00:18:54.967 --> 00:18:58.971
because we had seen
the IDFA forums, which

00:18:58.971 --> 00:19:00.222
seemed very intimidating.

00:19:00.222 --> 00:19:03.100
And the Hot Docs forums where
people like, oh, my god.

00:19:03.100 --> 00:19:03.600
It was--

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:04.017
Yeah.

00:19:04.017 --> 00:19:04.935
There were 500 people.

00:19:04.935 --> 00:19:06.812
We were so nervous.

00:19:06.812 --> 00:19:07.896
We were so nervous.

00:19:07.896 --> 00:19:11.650
And we were thinking, should--
so do we have to have a script,

00:19:11.650 --> 00:19:15.445
and do we have to be very formal
like a lot of these filmmakers

00:19:15.445 --> 00:19:19.575
from Europe who have
all of these credits.

00:19:19.575 --> 00:19:21.368
And I mean, it was
amazing because we

00:19:21.368 --> 00:19:25.372
were working with an incredible
team at EyeSteel Film.

00:19:25.372 --> 00:19:27.624
And the team there,
Miller and Bob,

00:19:27.624 --> 00:19:30.169
had done some
incredible pictures.

00:19:30.169 --> 00:19:32.129
I remember there's a
pitch they did in Hot Docs

00:19:32.129 --> 00:19:35.299
where the were wearing fireman's
suits and with the audience.

00:19:35.299 --> 00:19:37.885
Then they just stood up and
everyone was like, what?

00:19:37.885 --> 00:19:40.888
I can't remember what -
Everyone talks about that pitch.

00:19:40.888 --> 00:19:41.722
But I don't think--

00:19:41.722 --> 00:19:44.850
I don't know if anyone
remembers what the film was.

00:19:44.850 --> 00:19:47.269
Yeah but they were so
amazing at pitches.

00:19:47.269 --> 00:19:51.106
And I mean, we really discussed
what made a great pitch.

00:19:51.106 --> 00:19:56.945
And outside of the basics
of being able to say who,

00:19:56.945 --> 00:20:00.032
what, where, and to be able
to access to the character

00:20:00.032 --> 00:20:02.910
and what the team was,
what made a great pitch

00:20:02.910 --> 00:20:06.079
was leaning into your
authentic selves as a team

00:20:06.079 --> 00:20:10.584
and why you, as a team,
wanted to do this project.

00:20:10.584 --> 00:20:14.630
And for us, Softie, was
a story and a project

00:20:14.630 --> 00:20:16.256
that we were working
on because we had

00:20:16.256 --> 00:20:18.508
a deep love for our country.

00:20:18.508 --> 00:20:23.055
We had a lot of questions about
our complicated relationship

00:20:23.055 --> 00:20:25.390
with it.

00:20:25.390 --> 00:20:29.186
And that's what we leaned into.

00:20:29.186 --> 00:20:33.649
And as a first time producer
pitching at Hot Docs,

00:20:33.649 --> 00:20:35.442
I didn't have a list of credits.

00:20:35.442 --> 00:20:38.820
So actually, when we were
pitching at Hot Docs, we just--

00:20:38.820 --> 00:20:40.739
we leaned into authentic selves.

00:20:40.739 --> 00:20:43.242
And we gave ourselves roles.

00:20:43.242 --> 00:20:45.244
Like, I'm very enthusiastic.

00:20:45.244 --> 00:20:48.288
And we were happy to be there
as a first time filmmaker so we

00:20:48.288 --> 00:20:49.081
just--

00:20:49.081 --> 00:20:50.207
we riled up the crowd.

00:20:50.207 --> 00:20:52.876
And we're like, yeah, happy
to be here, like, shout out

00:20:52.876 --> 00:20:55.254
to Doc Society, shout
out to Just Film,

00:20:55.254 --> 00:20:58.632
shout out to all our partners,
and we love our country,

00:20:58.632 --> 00:20:59.424
we love being here.

00:20:59.424 --> 00:21:01.176
And I think everyone
was a bit shocked.

00:21:01.176 --> 00:21:02.803
They were like, oh,
my god the Africans

00:21:02.803 --> 00:21:05.180
are very like, expressive.

00:21:05.180 --> 00:21:07.557
You [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:07.557 --> 00:21:08.850
Everyone was like, so happy.

00:21:08.850 --> 00:21:11.687
And I think we came
with great energy

00:21:11.687 --> 00:21:15.607
because we really did not want
to be telling another story

00:21:15.607 --> 00:21:17.109
about how Africa--

00:21:17.109 --> 00:21:19.319
an African country has
a failed democracy.

00:21:19.319 --> 00:21:22.030
That was not the story
that we wanted to tell.

00:21:22.030 --> 00:21:25.701
We wanted to tell a story of
hope, a story of possibility.

00:21:25.701 --> 00:21:27.661
And we leaned into
that with our pitch.

00:21:27.661 --> 00:21:33.208
And Soko did a pitch where he
was talking over the trailer.

00:21:33.208 --> 00:21:36.295
So he was weaving in and out
of the trailer, which is risky.

00:21:36.295 --> 00:21:38.130
And it worked.

00:21:38.130 --> 00:21:41.216
And out of participating
in Hot Docs forum, which

00:21:41.216 --> 00:21:45.929
was very scary for us,
yeah, we got POV on board

00:21:45.929 --> 00:21:49.308
as a co-producing partner,
co-production partner.

00:21:49.308 --> 00:21:53.103
We got BBC Storyville later
down the line, Sundance.

00:21:53.103 --> 00:21:54.104
So I would say, Softie--

00:21:54.104 --> 00:21:55.522
You got it all.

00:21:55.522 --> 00:21:56.398
Yeah.

00:21:56.398 --> 00:21:57.190
I mean, not--

00:21:57.190 --> 00:21:58.859
You got the momentum.

00:21:58.859 --> 00:22:03.030
But it took six months,
six months to a year,

00:22:03.030 --> 00:22:04.781
of talking back and
forth with people

00:22:04.781 --> 00:22:06.950
who had expressed an interest.

00:22:06.950 --> 00:22:08.744
Because fundraising is about--

00:22:08.744 --> 00:22:10.746
it's also about-- it's
about establishing

00:22:10.746 --> 00:22:12.622
a relationship with someone.

00:22:12.622 --> 00:22:17.085
It's about getting someone to
be part of the team as a funder.

00:22:17.085 --> 00:22:18.712
We have been
fortunate that we had

00:22:18.712 --> 00:22:25.427
people doubling down their
commitment more than once.

00:22:25.427 --> 00:22:28.972
Doc Society doubled down
their commitment three times.

00:22:28.972 --> 00:22:30.724
And even supported impact.

00:22:30.724 --> 00:22:34.186
Luminate, our other funder,
doubled down their commitment.

00:22:34.186 --> 00:22:37.397
And that's because we didn't
treat them as an ATM machine.

00:22:37.397 --> 00:22:40.609
But we treated them as
partners, as collaborators.

00:22:40.609 --> 00:22:43.195
And we kept on having
conversations with them

00:22:43.195 --> 00:22:47.824
about what our vision
was for the documentary

00:22:47.824 --> 00:22:50.911
and for our audience
outreach and engagement.

00:22:50.911 --> 00:22:51.495
Yeah.

00:22:51.495 --> 00:22:53.830
I mean, you know,
let's talk a little bit

00:22:53.830 --> 00:22:57.918
about of breaking into the doc.

00:22:57.918 --> 00:23:02.547
Kenya, and what you guys have
done as in this nonfiction

00:23:02.547 --> 00:23:05.342
space in Kenya is remarkable.

00:23:05.342 --> 00:23:09.262
It's a game changer for
non-fiction in Africa.

00:23:09.262 --> 00:23:13.475
And what was-- when
I first met you guys,

00:23:13.475 --> 00:23:15.018
and I believe it was--

00:23:15.018 --> 00:23:20.690
I can't remember if it
was Hot Docs or IDFA,

00:23:20.690 --> 00:23:22.818
it was that enthusiasm.

00:23:22.818 --> 00:23:26.613
I remember like, you and Judy
Kibinge from the production,

00:23:26.613 --> 00:23:29.408
just coming up to me so excited.

00:23:29.408 --> 00:23:30.867
And like, we are so excited.

00:23:30.867 --> 00:23:31.827
We want to meet people.

00:23:31.827 --> 00:23:33.412
And I was like,
you guys are great.

00:23:33.412 --> 00:23:35.372
And I was like, you
want to meet people.

00:23:35.372 --> 00:23:36.331
And I pointed out--

00:23:36.331 --> 00:23:39.584
I remember we were in I think
it was Sheffield, the Sheffield

00:23:39.584 --> 00:23:40.252
Doc Fest.

00:23:40.252 --> 00:23:43.338
And I was pointing out
people in the room.

00:23:43.338 --> 00:23:45.632
And then you guys
would go up to them.

00:23:45.632 --> 00:23:47.884
And I like, that
person's important.

00:23:47.884 --> 00:23:49.177
That's Simon Kilmurry.

00:23:49.177 --> 00:23:51.096
He's head of the IDA.

00:23:51.096 --> 00:23:53.640
And you guys would go, OK,
I'm going to go up to him.

00:23:53.640 --> 00:23:54.641
Like, hey, Simon.

00:23:54.641 --> 00:23:58.019
And it's just so great.

00:23:58.019 --> 00:24:01.273
And what you've
done is you gained

00:24:01.273 --> 00:24:05.652
the respect of the industry.

00:24:05.652 --> 00:24:10.740
And you've charted
a way for, really,

00:24:10.740 --> 00:24:14.244
for African nonfiction
filmmakers to--

00:24:14.244 --> 00:24:17.038
people to take notice and
be like, wait a minute.

00:24:17.038 --> 00:24:19.040
Why are we going
there and telling

00:24:19.040 --> 00:24:22.335
these devastating
stories instead

00:24:22.335 --> 00:24:24.880
of embracing the stories
that we want to tell.

00:24:24.880 --> 00:24:30.469
But how is it navigating
the Kenyan film industry

00:24:30.469 --> 00:24:33.472
versus the Western
film industry?

00:24:33.472 --> 00:24:37.392
You know, because there's been
some issues around censorship,

00:24:37.392 --> 00:24:40.937
around all kinds of
problems in Kenya

00:24:40.937 --> 00:24:43.857
of getting your films released.

00:24:43.857 --> 00:24:44.357
Yeah.

00:24:44.357 --> 00:24:48.904
I mean, I could just speak a
little bit to our enthusiasm

00:24:48.904 --> 00:24:54.201
for being out there at
IDFA, Sheffield, Hot Docs.

00:24:54.201 --> 00:24:54.701
Listen.

00:24:54.701 --> 00:24:58.455
We, for a very long
time, people who

00:24:58.455 --> 00:25:01.082
look like me, people who
look like Soko, Pete,

00:25:01.082 --> 00:25:03.502
we're not telling our stories.

00:25:03.502 --> 00:25:07.923
And the reason why I
said earlier on like, I

00:25:07.923 --> 00:25:12.761
feel like it's coming full
circle, and I truly do mean it,

00:25:12.761 --> 00:25:16.473
is because for a
long time, we thought

00:25:16.473 --> 00:25:19.434
that creative documentaries
or documentaries that

00:25:19.434 --> 00:25:22.771
could be at A festivals,
or even fiction films that

00:25:22.771 --> 00:25:25.357
could be at A
festivals, that was

00:25:25.357 --> 00:25:29.736
the preserve of Western
filmmakers, largely

00:25:29.736 --> 00:25:33.073
white, male Western filmmakers.

00:25:33.073 --> 00:25:35.450
And it just felt
like something that

00:25:35.450 --> 00:25:40.580
was completely inaccessible for
us in so many different ways

00:25:40.580 --> 00:25:44.834
because we don't have
access to development

00:25:44.834 --> 00:25:47.796
funding for projects.

00:25:47.796 --> 00:25:49.589
For a long time,
we really did not

00:25:49.589 --> 00:25:55.011
have the skill set to really
be able to do, to produce,

00:25:55.011 --> 00:25:57.055
and direct creative
documentaries,

00:25:57.055 --> 00:25:59.474
and creative character-driven
documentaries.

00:25:59.474 --> 00:26:01.518
Soko, honestly, just kept
on filming and filming.

00:26:01.518 --> 00:26:03.895
And he had such an
incredible access.

00:26:03.895 --> 00:26:07.941
And he had such an
incredible subjects

00:26:07.941 --> 00:26:08.984
that he was filming with.

00:26:08.984 --> 00:26:11.820
And it was through
watching documentaries.

00:26:11.820 --> 00:26:14.906
It was through watching
these lectures, videos,

00:26:14.906 --> 00:26:16.783
we were watching about
Hot Docs and IDFA

00:26:16.783 --> 00:26:20.328
online, where we were mostly
seeing these white filmmakers

00:26:20.328 --> 00:26:21.538
talking about the process.

00:26:21.538 --> 00:26:26.167
So when we got there, we
were ready to take advantage

00:26:26.167 --> 00:26:28.086
of every single opportunity.

00:26:28.086 --> 00:26:31.006
Because we also
understood that this

00:26:31.006 --> 00:26:33.758
wasn't just about us
as a film team, but it

00:26:33.758 --> 00:26:36.011
was also just about
the next generation.

00:26:36.011 --> 00:26:37.554
I don't even see
the next generation.

00:26:37.554 --> 00:26:40.348
It was about the
next filmmakers who

00:26:40.348 --> 00:26:43.018
are going to come up
after us and those people

00:26:43.018 --> 00:26:45.020
who have projects
in post-production

00:26:45.020 --> 00:26:46.062
like Zippy Kimundu.

00:26:46.062 --> 00:26:48.732
She is doing an amazing
documentary about Mau Mau.

00:26:48.732 --> 00:26:51.109
So we were going to
kick the door open

00:26:51.109 --> 00:26:54.112
and leave it open so that
other people can come there.

00:26:54.112 --> 00:26:57.365
Because we don't want to
be the only people having

00:26:57.365 --> 00:26:58.617
a seat at the table.

00:26:58.617 --> 00:27:01.786
And we did not want to be
seen as these unicorns,

00:27:01.786 --> 00:27:04.914
these special Africans, who
can tell specific films.

00:27:04.914 --> 00:27:06.499
Because we wanted
people to understand

00:27:06.499 --> 00:27:08.376
there was more of
us coming there.

00:27:08.376 --> 00:27:10.128
So that's why we were
very enthusiastic.

00:27:10.128 --> 00:27:14.507
Because we knew that this
is an amazing opportunity

00:27:14.507 --> 00:27:18.970
that could potentially transform
our independent documentary

00:27:18.970 --> 00:27:21.765
industry in Kenya.

00:27:21.765 --> 00:27:24.017
So that's why there's
all this enthusiasm

00:27:24.017 --> 00:27:26.519
with that project and all the
other projects that we do.

00:27:26.519 --> 00:27:29.105
Because we do truly
feel like we're

00:27:29.105 --> 00:27:33.401
in a very special moment, where
we are finally having access,

00:27:33.401 --> 00:27:39.032
and we're finally talking to the
right people at the, probably,

00:27:39.032 --> 00:27:41.660
right room, maybe not the right
table but probably, right room.

00:27:41.660 --> 00:27:43.495
But that's OK because
before, we didn't even

00:27:43.495 --> 00:27:45.872
know the room existed.

00:27:45.872 --> 00:27:48.708
And I would say navigating
the Kenyan industry

00:27:48.708 --> 00:27:53.004
when it comes to censorship,
I would see a lot of that.

00:27:53.004 --> 00:27:55.840
I mean, it's difficult
right now in Kenya

00:27:55.840 --> 00:28:01.054
because Softie got an 18
classification with the Kenyan

00:28:01.054 --> 00:28:03.264
Film and Censorship Board.

00:28:03.264 --> 00:28:04.974
They said-- yeah, it did.

00:28:04.974 --> 00:28:05.475
It did.

00:28:05.475 --> 00:28:05.975
It did.

00:28:05.975 --> 00:28:07.727
What is this 18 classification?

00:28:07.727 --> 00:28:09.104
What does that mean?

00:28:09.104 --> 00:28:09.854
18 plus.

00:28:09.854 --> 00:28:13.858
So only people over the age
of 18 could watch in theater--

00:28:13.858 --> 00:28:14.818
Why is that?

00:28:14.818 --> 00:28:17.779
--or TV in its current format.

00:28:17.779 --> 00:28:20.490
And the reasons given
was police brutality,

00:28:20.490 --> 00:28:24.077
which I thought was amazing.

00:28:24.077 --> 00:28:27.414
And you know, they talked
about post-election violence.

00:28:27.414 --> 00:28:29.916
A lot of the stuff
had been on TV.

00:28:29.916 --> 00:28:33.545
They couldn't outright ban it
because we've been so public

00:28:33.545 --> 00:28:36.131
about our release at Sundance.

00:28:36.131 --> 00:28:37.799
And we kept making
noise about it.

00:28:37.799 --> 00:28:39.551
Because that was our strategy.

00:28:39.551 --> 00:28:42.053
Let's make as much noise
as possible about the film.

00:28:42.053 --> 00:28:43.513
Let's be enthusiastic.

00:28:43.513 --> 00:28:46.683
Let's get everyone talking
so that-- let's just

00:28:46.683 --> 00:28:50.270
make it so difficult for anyone
to even try and ban this film.

00:28:50.270 --> 00:28:52.647
Because we wanted it to be seen.

00:28:52.647 --> 00:28:53.940
And that was just our strategy.

00:28:53.940 --> 00:28:54.607
Let's be loud.

00:28:54.607 --> 00:28:55.275
Let's be noisy.

00:28:55.275 --> 00:28:58.194
Let's celebrate
as much as we can

00:28:58.194 --> 00:29:01.406
because who wants to
be a party pooper.

00:29:01.406 --> 00:29:03.074
So I think that was our--

00:29:03.074 --> 00:29:05.535
that was our strategy with
dealing with censorship.

00:29:05.535 --> 00:29:09.330
And distribution is
another challenge in Kenya.

00:29:09.330 --> 00:29:12.709
We really had to--

00:29:12.709 --> 00:29:17.672
I mean, we really, really had
to lobby the local distributor,

00:29:17.672 --> 00:29:21.885
Crimson, to be able to even
just look at the documentary.

00:29:21.885 --> 00:29:25.096
Because in Kenya, when people
think about documentaries,

00:29:25.096 --> 00:29:28.391
they think that it's
about water, building

00:29:28.391 --> 00:29:29.934
a well, or something like that.

00:29:29.934 --> 00:29:30.435
Right.

00:29:30.435 --> 00:29:32.103
They forgot to think
that it's engaging.

00:29:32.103 --> 00:29:34.522
We had to keep on calling the
distributor and telling her,

00:29:34.522 --> 00:29:35.565
hey, have you watched it?

00:29:35.565 --> 00:29:36.608
Hey, have you watched it?

00:29:36.608 --> 00:29:39.110
So she just finally watched
it after she got tired

00:29:39.110 --> 00:29:40.069
of me calling her.

00:29:40.069 --> 00:29:41.863
And when she watched, it,
she's like, oh, my god.

00:29:41.863 --> 00:29:42.530
This is amazing.

00:29:42.530 --> 00:29:44.115
We need to get in
all the theaters.

00:29:44.115 --> 00:29:48.495
And I think that--
so I think for us,

00:29:48.495 --> 00:29:52.290
because we're in the phase where
we're kind of pioneers in Kenya

00:29:52.290 --> 00:29:56.127
when it comes to
independent documentaries,

00:29:56.127 --> 00:29:57.712
theatrical documentaries--

00:29:57.712 --> 00:29:59.214
because people have
done other sorts

00:29:59.214 --> 00:30:01.800
of documentaries and fiction--

00:30:01.800 --> 00:30:03.218
there's a lot of
education that we

00:30:03.218 --> 00:30:06.888
need to do in our generation.

00:30:06.888 --> 00:30:10.850
And we're happy to do
it because we absolutely

00:30:10.850 --> 00:30:13.770
love documentary storytelling.

00:30:13.770 --> 00:30:16.773
I've found it very
transformative in my life.

00:30:16.773 --> 00:30:18.942
Yeah.

00:30:18.942 --> 00:30:23.404
So you're educating people
all around, both sides.

00:30:23.404 --> 00:30:27.617
You're educating the Kenyans.

00:30:27.617 --> 00:30:28.910
Yeah.

00:30:28.910 --> 00:30:30.036
Yeah.

00:30:30.036 --> 00:30:31.329
Incredible.

00:30:31.329 --> 00:30:34.165
And you mentioned a
lot of organizations.

00:30:34.165 --> 00:30:36.334
And I think that it's
important for filmmakers

00:30:36.334 --> 00:30:37.961
to understand some
of the opportunities

00:30:37.961 --> 00:30:40.421
in these organizations.

00:30:40.421 --> 00:30:42.006
I believe I met you--

00:30:42.006 --> 00:30:44.384
I think I met you,
it was Blue Ice.

00:30:44.384 --> 00:30:44.884
Yes.

00:30:44.884 --> 00:30:49.180
It was Blue Ice
where you and Pete

00:30:49.180 --> 00:30:51.683
were working on I am Samuel.

00:30:51.683 --> 00:30:56.187
And I remember I
saw that work, and I

00:30:56.187 --> 00:30:59.148
saw both of your enthusiasm.

00:30:59.148 --> 00:31:00.942
And I was like, I'm signing on.

00:31:00.942 --> 00:31:05.280
I want to be executive producer
I want to work with these guys.

00:31:05.280 --> 00:31:07.615
So talk about some
of the opportunities

00:31:07.615 --> 00:31:15.415
available to filmmakers in Kenya
like Blue Ice, and of course,

00:31:15.415 --> 00:31:18.334
the amazing DocuBox.

00:31:18.334 --> 00:31:19.252
Yes.

00:31:19.252 --> 00:31:23.381
I think right now is
an incredible time

00:31:23.381 --> 00:31:29.095
to be an independent
storyteller from continent.

00:31:29.095 --> 00:31:31.389
Because I think
right now there's

00:31:31.389 --> 00:31:34.726
an increased interest
in diverse stories.

00:31:34.726 --> 00:31:37.395
A lot of people committed
to that across the board.

00:31:37.395 --> 00:31:39.397
So I'm just like,
if you have an idea,

00:31:39.397 --> 00:31:41.566
and you want to get it done,
I think now's the time.

00:31:41.566 --> 00:31:43.234
Don't wait.

00:31:43.234 --> 00:31:48.489
So I would say, organizations
that you could reach out to

00:31:48.489 --> 00:31:51.993
for production funding
would be DocuBox

00:31:51.993 --> 00:31:53.161
which is based in Kenya.

00:31:53.161 --> 00:31:57.624
It's East Africa's first
independent documentary film

00:31:57.624 --> 00:31:58.833
fund.

00:31:58.833 --> 00:32:04.130
Then there's also DocA
which is Africa-wide.

00:32:04.130 --> 00:32:07.467
They have development funding.

00:32:07.467 --> 00:32:10.678
They also have
production funding

00:32:10.678 --> 00:32:13.598
I know they have impact
distribution funding.

00:32:13.598 --> 00:32:15.183
Outside of that,
they have their funds

00:32:15.183 --> 00:32:18.394
outside of the continent
like Hot Doc's Blue Ice

00:32:18.394 --> 00:32:23.149
that's interested in supporting
African storytellers.

00:32:23.149 --> 00:32:28.321
Both my projects Softie and I am
Samuel got funding from there.

00:32:28.321 --> 00:32:30.448
And I know so many other
filmmakers who got that.

00:32:30.448 --> 00:32:35.119
There's IDFA as
well and Sundance.

00:32:35.119 --> 00:32:40.291
Sundance has also just
refocused its fund

00:32:40.291 --> 00:32:43.503
to finance documentary
storytellers

00:32:43.503 --> 00:32:47.715
from the communities where
they're telling the stories.

00:32:47.715 --> 00:32:49.842
So that means, no more--

00:32:49.842 --> 00:32:52.095
they're interested
in really supporting

00:32:52.095 --> 00:32:56.099
parachute filmmaking,
as I like to call it.

00:32:56.099 --> 00:32:57.892
Exactly.

00:32:57.892 --> 00:33:00.228
Comes in and tells
the story and leaves.

00:33:00.228 --> 00:33:01.938
I mean, they want
someone who actually

00:33:01.938 --> 00:33:03.982
has some level of
connection and understanding

00:33:03.982 --> 00:33:06.317
of their communities, which
is why I'm just saying,

00:33:06.317 --> 00:33:10.697
right now is a perfect time
to be developing stories.

00:33:10.697 --> 00:33:11.197
Yeah.

00:33:11.197 --> 00:33:14.826
I'd say grants, Ford Foundation.

00:33:14.826 --> 00:33:15.326
Yeah.

00:33:15.326 --> 00:33:18.037
I would say-- I would say,
as an African storyteller

00:33:18.037 --> 00:33:21.708
at this point,
it's mostly grants.

00:33:21.708 --> 00:33:25.253
It's mostly grant organizations,
so specific documentary grant

00:33:25.253 --> 00:33:27.839
organizations like the one
I talked about and also

00:33:27.839 --> 00:33:29.966
thematic funding organizations.

00:33:29.966 --> 00:33:32.010
Because they could
be an organization

00:33:32.010 --> 00:33:35.138
that doesn't directly
support documentary.

00:33:35.138 --> 00:33:38.016
But maybe it's a climate
change organization.

00:33:38.016 --> 00:33:40.351
And you could be
able to convince

00:33:40.351 --> 00:33:46.149
them to finance your documentary
or to support it and later

00:33:46.149 --> 00:33:51.946
on support its distribution and
in their target communities.

00:33:51.946 --> 00:33:54.198
That's, I think, one of the
interesting ways you could

00:33:54.198 --> 00:33:55.450
be able to raise financing.

00:33:58.119 --> 00:34:01.289
And what's really
interesting, what's changing,

00:34:01.289 --> 00:34:04.125
now is there are now
commercial opportunities.

00:34:04.125 --> 00:34:10.256
And now huge streamers like
Netflix are on the continent

00:34:10.256 --> 00:34:12.341
and looking for content.

00:34:12.341 --> 00:34:16.846
And that gives an
opportunity for filmmakers

00:34:16.846 --> 00:34:22.477
to actually make real money and
not be just reliant on grants.

00:34:22.477 --> 00:34:26.022
So maybe talk about
that a little bit.

00:34:26.022 --> 00:34:26.856
Yeah.

00:34:26.856 --> 00:34:30.151
I think, yeah, I mean,
I do know that they

00:34:30.151 --> 00:34:35.406
have come into Africa in the
fiction space, specifically.

00:34:35.406 --> 00:34:37.283
I know a couple of filmmakers.

00:34:37.283 --> 00:34:41.704
There's this Nigerian director
called Moolaadé, who's had--

00:34:41.704 --> 00:34:43.456
actually, she has a
film that's coming out

00:34:43.456 --> 00:34:44.999
of Netflix quite soon.

00:34:44.999 --> 00:34:50.379
There's a few Kenyan
directors Toshi Tonga,

00:34:50.379 --> 00:34:52.965
executive producer Fakii
Liwali who's Kenyan,

00:34:52.965 --> 00:34:55.510
and the Nigerian filmmakers
and South African

00:34:55.510 --> 00:35:00.973
filmmakers who have had either
originals or acquisitions done

00:35:00.973 --> 00:35:01.474
by Netflix.

00:35:01.474 --> 00:35:05.853
So it's quite exciting that
they're coming into this space.

00:35:05.853 --> 00:35:09.148
And I'm sure they'll coming
to documentary quite soon.

00:35:09.148 --> 00:35:12.527
Because it's true what you're
saying, grants are great,

00:35:12.527 --> 00:35:14.737
but they're highly competitive.

00:35:14.737 --> 00:35:17.073
So it's good to be
able to diversify

00:35:17.073 --> 00:35:21.077
your sources of financing.

00:35:21.077 --> 00:35:24.163
And I think we're very
excited about that, the fact

00:35:24.163 --> 00:35:25.623
that they've come
to the continent.

00:35:25.623 --> 00:35:29.627
And hopefully,
hopefully, HBO and Amazon

00:35:29.627 --> 00:35:31.921
and all the other people
are going to follow.

00:35:31.921 --> 00:35:32.463
Yeah.

00:35:32.463 --> 00:35:39.262
I mean, there's, with the global
reckoning on race, there is a--

00:35:39.262 --> 00:35:42.974
parachute filmmaking, as
you call it, isn't really--

00:35:42.974 --> 00:35:45.601
no one tolerates that anymore.

00:35:45.601 --> 00:35:48.479
You know, they can't get
away with that anymore.

00:35:48.479 --> 00:35:50.273
And those people
who parachute in

00:35:50.273 --> 00:35:52.900
to tell the stories
of Africa, those films

00:35:52.900 --> 00:35:56.320
are not getting they're not--
they're not getting green-lit.

00:35:56.320 --> 00:35:57.738
They're not getting seen.

00:35:57.738 --> 00:36:00.324
And that's because of the
work that you guys have done.

00:36:00.324 --> 00:36:03.536
Because people realize that
superior work is coming out

00:36:03.536 --> 00:36:04.245
of--

00:36:04.245 --> 00:36:07.832
from the filmmakers who
lived those experiences

00:36:07.832 --> 00:36:12.170
You know, I do want to talk
a little bit about the change

00:36:12.170 --> 00:36:17.049
in Kenya with the government
and the official change

00:36:17.049 --> 00:36:20.511
and the way people
perceive nonfiction.

00:36:20.511 --> 00:36:25.641
Because this year, The Letter,
the documentary The Letter,

00:36:25.641 --> 00:36:28.936
was the Kenyan
submission to the Oscars.

00:36:28.936 --> 00:36:30.938
I mean, how could that--

00:36:30.938 --> 00:36:32.857
that must have been--

00:36:32.857 --> 00:36:34.859
that was groundbreaking.

00:36:34.859 --> 00:36:36.569
Oh, that was incredible.

00:36:36.569 --> 00:36:41.240
Like, we were so happy for
the directing team, Maria

00:36:41.240 --> 00:36:43.201
and Chris.

00:36:43.201 --> 00:36:46.329
Yeah Because yeah, we
have filmmaking community

00:36:46.329 --> 00:36:47.455
like in East Africa.

00:36:47.455 --> 00:36:49.373
So we're always talking
about our stories

00:36:49.373 --> 00:36:51.542
and sharing our experiences
with each other.

00:36:51.542 --> 00:36:55.755
So we were so happy about that.

00:36:55.755 --> 00:36:57.548
And there was a lot
of encouragement

00:36:57.548 --> 00:37:01.802
from other filmmakers who
had gone through this Oscar

00:37:01.802 --> 00:37:04.138
committee selection process
at a national level,

00:37:04.138 --> 00:37:07.183
to be able to encourage
them on how to apply.

00:37:07.183 --> 00:37:13.189
And the fact that they got
chosen was a validation of--

00:37:13.189 --> 00:37:17.443
a validation in Kenya that
documentary is cinema.

00:37:17.443 --> 00:37:20.821
I mean, I know that, but a
lot of people don't know that.

00:37:20.821 --> 00:37:24.408
So I do know a lot of people who
are like, huh, a documentary.

00:37:24.408 --> 00:37:30.456
And they did a theatrical
release after their selection.

00:37:30.456 --> 00:37:33.417
And so many people went
to watch the documentary.

00:37:33.417 --> 00:37:36.921
And so many people were
blown away by their access,

00:37:36.921 --> 00:37:40.216
by their storytelling
I mean, Maia and Chris

00:37:40.216 --> 00:37:43.052
have so many stories to tell
about their filming process.

00:37:43.052 --> 00:37:46.847
And it's such a beautiful,
nuanced documentary.

00:37:46.847 --> 00:37:50.476
And I'm so glad
that people actually

00:37:50.476 --> 00:37:52.770
got to see it in theaters.

00:37:52.770 --> 00:37:55.189
People are continuing to see it.

00:37:55.189 --> 00:37:55.690
Yeah.

00:37:55.690 --> 00:37:58.651
I just wish they'd gotten
support for the Awards, right?

00:37:58.651 --> 00:38:02.071
But we're hoping that next
year, the Kenyan submission

00:38:02.071 --> 00:38:04.573
will get some support
for the Awards from.

00:38:04.573 --> 00:38:05.074
Yeah.

00:38:05.074 --> 00:38:05.616
Incredible.

00:38:05.616 --> 00:38:06.951
Incredible.

00:38:06.951 --> 00:38:11.205
Well, before we run out of time,
I want to get to some questions

00:38:11.205 --> 00:38:14.000
from student filmmakers.

00:38:14.000 --> 00:38:16.669
So the first question
is, what does it

00:38:16.669 --> 00:38:20.881
mean to "develop"
a documentary idea,

00:38:20.881 --> 00:38:24.010
and what steps are
involved and when

00:38:24.010 --> 00:38:27.972
do the idea is ready to pitch?

00:38:27.972 --> 00:38:28.472
Wow.

00:38:28.472 --> 00:38:28.973
OK.

00:38:28.973 --> 00:38:30.224
I'll try and be brief.

00:38:33.436 --> 00:38:34.020
There's a lot there.

00:38:34.020 --> 00:38:34.186
OK.

00:38:34.186 --> 00:38:36.564
I'll try and be as
succinct as possible.

00:38:37.315 --> 00:38:46.407
I would say development is
testing your thesis statement.

00:38:46.407 --> 00:38:48.701
You might think you
might have an idea

00:38:48.701 --> 00:38:53.247
and say, oh, I want to do
something about like farmers

00:38:53.247 --> 00:38:57.293
and how climate change actually
affects them in rural villages.

00:38:57.293 --> 00:38:58.919
So that could just
be the general idea.

00:38:58.919 --> 00:39:02.923
And then what you would
do is probably gather

00:39:02.923 --> 00:39:04.759
a team, maybe a cameraman
and sound person.

00:39:04.759 --> 00:39:06.344
Or you would go
yourself with, maybe,

00:39:06.344 --> 00:39:09.013
a small camera, your
phone, and go and interview

00:39:09.013 --> 00:39:12.725
different people to try and
maybe get a farmer to follow,

00:39:12.725 --> 00:39:17.563
maybe interview some experts,
and then also do some research

00:39:17.563 --> 00:39:20.232
as you're filming or even
before you go into the field

00:39:20.232 --> 00:39:26.530
just to understand, is this does
this idea have any validity,

00:39:26.530 --> 00:39:29.950
are there areas that we
could explore further.

00:39:29.950 --> 00:39:32.578
And then I would just say it's
just continuing that process.

00:39:32.578 --> 00:39:38.000
And I think when you feel
like you have a few hours,

00:39:38.000 --> 00:39:42.088
I would say, 15 to
30 hours of filming,

00:39:42.088 --> 00:39:46.175
you could review your footage
and try and edit, maybe,

00:39:46.175 --> 00:39:50.554
something small that you think
summarizes the who, what,

00:39:50.554 --> 00:39:53.766
where, why part of the story.

00:39:53.766 --> 00:39:54.642
And yeah.

00:39:54.642 --> 00:39:58.104
You would also accompany that
with a write-up, a proposal.

00:39:58.104 --> 00:40:00.606
And there's lots of
resources online where

00:40:00.606 --> 00:40:02.149
you can be able
to just understand

00:40:02.149 --> 00:40:05.319
how to create these specific
things that I'm talking about

00:40:05.319 --> 00:40:07.822
because I'm just talking
about it in brief.

00:40:07.822 --> 00:40:10.825
There's lots of
online resources.

00:40:10.825 --> 00:40:13.744
And Sundance Collab has
a lot of free master

00:40:13.744 --> 00:40:17.540
classes on how to actually
develop documentaries.

00:40:17.540 --> 00:40:19.625
And I would urge you
to go there and just

00:40:19.625 --> 00:40:21.127
look at that in detail.

00:40:21.127 --> 00:40:21.877
Yeah

00:40:21.877 --> 00:40:22.545
Yeah.

00:40:22.545 --> 00:40:26.257
I've done the Collab
master class myself.

00:40:26.257 --> 00:40:30.344
Next question is what
is it like to show

00:40:30.344 --> 00:40:33.681
your film at international
film festivals like Sundance?

00:40:33.681 --> 00:40:37.309
And what does a producer
do at a festival?

00:40:37.309 --> 00:40:38.853
OK.

00:40:38.853 --> 00:40:41.147
Yes I will try and keep
this brief as well.

00:40:41.147 --> 00:40:42.064
Yeah.

00:40:42.064 --> 00:40:49.864
It was amazing to just be
at this A-list festival

00:40:49.864 --> 00:40:53.117
that we never even
dreamed we could ever

00:40:53.117 --> 00:40:55.661
have a film in competitions.

00:40:55.661 --> 00:40:57.538
That was just incredible.

00:40:57.538 --> 00:41:00.416
And we made sure we took the
time to live in the moment.

00:41:00.416 --> 00:41:04.253
We even had an African-themed
party at Sundance

00:41:04.253 --> 00:41:06.797
because there was a number of
African documentaries and films

00:41:06.797 --> 00:41:07.465
that were there.

00:41:07.465 --> 00:41:11.135
So we had a Kenyan
DJ, a Nigerian DJ.

00:41:11.135 --> 00:41:13.304
So we were the best party
at Sundance last year.

00:41:13.304 --> 00:41:15.931
I heard that .

00:41:15.931 --> 00:41:17.224
I'm sorry I missed it.

00:41:17.224 --> 00:41:18.517
We were the best party.

00:41:18.517 --> 00:41:20.394
And we played music everywhere.

00:41:20.394 --> 00:41:23.314
So people understood that there
were different kinds of music

00:41:23.314 --> 00:41:27.234
and cultural nuances in Africa.

00:41:27.234 --> 00:41:29.612
We're not-- we're not a country.

00:41:29.612 --> 00:41:35.034
And then I would say, outside
of that, I think at a festival,

00:41:35.034 --> 00:41:37.745
my work specifically,
was fundraising,

00:41:37.745 --> 00:41:41.040
to ensure that we could take
the subject and the film team

00:41:41.040 --> 00:41:42.750
to the festival.

00:41:42.750 --> 00:41:45.252
So going back to our
partners and reaching out

00:41:45.252 --> 00:41:47.004
to new partners to support us.

00:41:47.004 --> 00:41:49.089
And then it was
organizing the premiere.

00:41:49.089 --> 00:41:50.591
Because you have
to do a guest list.

00:41:50.591 --> 00:41:54.595
You have to make sure all
materials are delivered,

00:41:54.595 --> 00:41:56.430
and then working
with a publicity team

00:41:56.430 --> 00:42:00.100
to make sure that you're
getting the right kind of press

00:42:00.100 --> 00:42:01.352
for your project.

00:42:01.352 --> 00:42:04.563
And also if you have a
sales agent at that point,

00:42:04.563 --> 00:42:07.775
it's just making
sure distributors,

00:42:07.775 --> 00:42:11.695
broadcasters are actually
watching your documentary.

00:42:11.695 --> 00:42:12.196
Yeah.

00:42:12.196 --> 00:42:16.617
And then I'd say, outside
of that, yeah, having fun.

00:42:16.617 --> 00:42:17.576
Yeah.

00:42:17.576 --> 00:42:20.037
Because your first
premiere only happens once.

00:42:20.037 --> 00:42:23.374
Everyone-- everyone
talks about how--

00:42:23.374 --> 00:42:25.000
you met everyone at Sundance.

00:42:25.000 --> 00:42:26.168
I don't know how you did it.

00:42:26.168 --> 00:42:27.711
But everybody would
come to me and be

00:42:27.711 --> 00:42:30.464
like, I met Toni and Sam.

00:42:30.464 --> 00:42:30.965
Everyone.

00:42:30.965 --> 00:42:32.174
We were having so much fun.

00:42:32.174 --> 00:42:32.675
Yeah.

00:42:32.675 --> 00:42:34.009
You guys--

00:42:34.009 --> 00:42:36.095
We were tired, but
we had so much fun.

00:42:36.095 --> 00:42:36.679
Yeah, yeah.

00:42:36.679 --> 00:42:37.179
Right.

00:42:37.179 --> 00:42:39.265
Yeah.

00:42:39.265 --> 00:42:41.475
Next question is
what types of stories

00:42:41.475 --> 00:42:43.686
do you think we can
tell as refugees

00:42:43.686 --> 00:42:46.438
living in camps in Kenya?

00:42:46.438 --> 00:42:48.899
Wow.

00:42:48.899 --> 00:42:50.359
I would say--

00:42:50.359 --> 00:42:52.820
I mean, that's a great question.

00:42:52.820 --> 00:42:57.283
I would say as a refugee
living in a camp in Kenya,

00:42:57.283 --> 00:42:59.910
my. working assumption is that
it's other people telling you

00:42:59.910 --> 00:43:06.917
stories I would say,
it's maybe looking

00:43:06.917 --> 00:43:11.380
at what you find interesting
about your life, that maybe

00:43:11.380 --> 00:43:14.466
doesn't fit into the narrative
that you think has been created

00:43:14.466 --> 00:43:17.219
about your own experience.

00:43:17.219 --> 00:43:18.929
Because I mean, I want to--

00:43:18.929 --> 00:43:20.639
I'm sure, I'm sure
people fall in love.

00:43:20.639 --> 00:43:25.936
I'm sure there's interesting
dynamic between you

00:43:25.936 --> 00:43:26.979
and your siblings.

00:43:26.979 --> 00:43:28.314
I'm not trying to be simplistic.

00:43:28.314 --> 00:43:30.441
But I'm just saying,
like, just understanding

00:43:30.441 --> 00:43:34.528
what it means from
your perspective,

00:43:34.528 --> 00:43:39.158
to be living in that place
and in this point in time.

00:43:39.158 --> 00:43:41.994
So I would just be like
look inwards a little bit

00:43:41.994 --> 00:43:45.539
just in terms of what
you think is interesting

00:43:45.539 --> 00:43:48.876
and what appeals to you
about your experience

00:43:48.876 --> 00:43:51.587
or about your friends
living in the camp.

00:43:51.587 --> 00:43:54.423
And you could just start
with a short story.

00:43:54.423 --> 00:43:56.967
There's lots of short stories
that you-- short documentaries

00:43:56.967 --> 00:43:59.970
you can find online.

00:43:59.970 --> 00:44:01.889
I would urge you
to look at Topic.

00:44:01.889 --> 00:44:04.808
It has amazing
short documentaries.

00:44:04.808 --> 00:44:09.605
New York Times: Op-Docs has
amazing short documentaries.

00:44:09.605 --> 00:44:12.274
If I think of other ideas,
I will read them up.

00:44:12.274 --> 00:44:14.943
But if you start looking
for short documentaries,

00:44:14.943 --> 00:44:16.528
that will take a
short amount of time.

00:44:16.528 --> 00:44:18.864
I would say, just
start researching

00:44:18.864 --> 00:44:22.159
and find things that
spark your interest.

00:44:22.159 --> 00:44:24.870
Because I think it's
important that you,

00:44:24.870 --> 00:44:28.582
as people who live
in the refugee camps,

00:44:28.582 --> 00:44:30.084
ensure that you
tell a story that

00:44:30.084 --> 00:44:33.462
shows the different dimensions
of what it means to be you,

00:44:33.462 --> 00:44:37.466
that there isn't one story,
that you're not just victims,

00:44:37.466 --> 00:44:39.718
you're not just
displaced people,

00:44:39.718 --> 00:44:42.346
your people's hopes and
dreams, who are complicated

00:44:42.346 --> 00:44:48.769
nuanced, and have the right
to share your own story.

00:44:48.769 --> 00:44:49.603
Yeah.

00:44:49.603 --> 00:44:50.938
Absolutely, absolutely.

00:44:50.938 --> 00:44:54.483
And I think that's good advice
that sort of, watching shorts.

00:44:59.113 --> 00:45:02.241
I have a series on
Topic, on shorts.

00:45:02.241 --> 00:45:04.118
BIPOC filmmakers from
around the world.

00:45:04.118 --> 00:45:05.411
And I show their short films.

00:45:05.411 --> 00:45:09.415
And I think that just to watch--

00:45:09.415 --> 00:45:14.461
just watch someone can
accomplish in storytelling,

00:45:14.461 --> 00:45:17.047
seeing what can be accomplished
in a short amount of time,

00:45:17.047 --> 00:45:20.551
whether it's 5 minutes or
15 minutes or 30 minutes,

00:45:20.551 --> 00:45:24.722
it's just powerful,
powerful, powerful.

00:45:24.722 --> 00:45:26.849
And you can do that
with little resources.

00:45:26.849 --> 00:45:32.646
So it's just a great
way or place to begin.

00:45:32.646 --> 00:45:33.147
OK.

00:45:33.147 --> 00:45:34.106
Two more questions.

00:45:34.106 --> 00:45:35.482
We're running out of time.

00:45:35.482 --> 00:45:39.403
I'm going to-- so more women
are writing and directing

00:45:39.403 --> 00:45:45.701
movies and series, do you think
we will see more Kenyan women

00:45:45.701 --> 00:45:48.036
filmmakers soon?

00:45:48.036 --> 00:45:48.537
OK.

00:45:48.537 --> 00:45:51.665
So that's an
interesting question.

00:45:51.665 --> 00:45:56.754
Actually, Kenya is an anomaly
because across the world,

00:45:56.754 --> 00:45:59.673
the film industry tends
to be male-dominated.

00:45:59.673 --> 00:46:03.135
But in Kenya, a lot of the
directors and producers

00:46:03.135 --> 00:46:04.136
are women.

00:46:04.136 --> 00:46:09.349
We have a long history of women
filmmakers being pioneers.

00:46:09.349 --> 00:46:11.477
There's Anne
Mungai, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:46:11.477 --> 00:46:13.896
There is Judy Kibenge
who founded DocuBox

00:46:13.896 --> 00:46:16.482
who also directed fiction.

00:46:16.482 --> 00:46:19.693
There's Njeri Karago who
was a Hollywood executive

00:46:19.693 --> 00:46:22.446
and came back to
produce documentaries.

00:46:22.446 --> 00:46:26.450
There's Wanuri
Kahiu, with Rafiki.

00:46:26.450 --> 00:46:28.702
So I would just say
that we're quite

00:46:28.702 --> 00:46:30.746
fortunate to be an anomaly.

00:46:30.746 --> 00:46:34.750
Because there is some level of
gender parity in this country.

00:46:34.750 --> 00:46:38.086
And I think it will only
get better and better.

00:46:38.086 --> 00:46:39.213
Why do you think that is?

00:46:39.213 --> 00:46:41.590
Why do you think so
many Kenyan women are

00:46:41.590 --> 00:46:45.677
directing films and succeeding?

00:46:45.677 --> 00:46:48.263
You know, oh, god,
I'm going to--

00:46:48.263 --> 00:46:49.807
I'm going to get in
trouble for this.

00:46:49.807 --> 00:46:53.101
But I think it's--

00:46:53.101 --> 00:46:54.645
well, I think it's--

00:46:54.645 --> 00:46:58.273
I mean, it's interesting
because when,

00:46:58.273 --> 00:47:02.820
you know, when people were first
doing documentaries and film,

00:47:02.820 --> 00:47:05.030
it tended to be NGO-funded.

00:47:07.366 --> 00:47:11.578
And I guess, that to a certain--

00:47:11.578 --> 00:47:14.540
I mean, the first people who
started directing and producing

00:47:14.540 --> 00:47:16.500
those projects,
tended to be women.

00:47:16.500 --> 00:47:20.629
So it was gender based issues.

00:47:20.629 --> 00:47:23.632
So I think that that's where
it comes from a certain degree.

00:47:23.632 --> 00:47:26.635
And outside of that, I
would just say it's--

00:47:26.635 --> 00:47:29.555
my generation, in
my 30s, it's also

00:47:29.555 --> 00:47:34.434
just because I saw women
like Njeri Karago produce.

00:47:34.434 --> 00:47:36.728
And I knew it was possible.

00:47:36.728 --> 00:47:38.772
I didn't have those
gender barriers

00:47:38.772 --> 00:47:41.942
where it was just men
producing in my country.

00:47:41.942 --> 00:47:44.611
So I think it just it was
just possible for women

00:47:44.611 --> 00:47:47.573
to produce and
make me-- and men,

00:47:47.573 --> 00:47:50.409
I think for some reason,
the men who were pioneers

00:47:50.409 --> 00:47:52.452
in the industry,
tended to gravitate

00:47:52.452 --> 00:47:55.330
towards like
cinematography, editing.

00:47:55.330 --> 00:47:58.125
Because I guess, they
had toys to play with.

00:47:58.125 --> 00:48:00.294
But I'm not really sure
what the reason is.

00:48:00.294 --> 00:48:01.461
Those are just my oppinions.

00:48:01.461 --> 00:48:04.715
Well, I think it's
something like the strong,

00:48:04.715 --> 00:48:10.012
powerful African woman,
the backbone of every facet

00:48:10.012 --> 00:48:15.934
of society, you know, from
carrying water - to...

00:48:15.934 --> 00:48:16.435
Yeah.

00:48:16.435 --> 00:48:17.269
That's that.

00:48:17.269 --> 00:48:18.520
That's that.

00:48:18.520 --> 00:48:19.646
We're the organizers.

00:48:19.646 --> 00:48:20.522
We're the organizers.

00:48:20.522 --> 00:48:21.565
Yeah, yeah.

00:48:21.565 --> 00:48:22.649
So it makes sense.

00:48:22.649 --> 00:48:25.527
And same thing in the
documentary in the West.

00:48:25.527 --> 00:48:28.572
I'd see now many
women are really

00:48:28.572 --> 00:48:31.825
incredible force in the
documentary industry

00:48:31.825 --> 00:48:34.161
in the West too.

00:48:34.161 --> 00:48:35.287
So OK.

00:48:35.287 --> 00:48:36.496
So we're running out of time.

00:48:36.496 --> 00:48:42.419
Last question for you is
who are your role models

00:48:42.419 --> 00:48:47.424
and what filmmakers
inspire you the most?

00:48:47.424 --> 00:48:50.010
Oh, wow.

00:48:50.010 --> 00:48:52.346
That is such a
difficult question.

00:48:54.723 --> 00:48:55.474
Wow.

00:48:55.474 --> 00:48:58.268
You should be-- we should,
how should filmmakers

00:48:58.268 --> 00:49:01.813
be sort of looking out
for inspiration if they

00:49:01.813 --> 00:49:10.447
want to watch the films,
you know, and be inspired.

00:49:10.447 --> 00:49:12.240
Who do you turn to?

00:49:12.240 --> 00:49:12.741
OK.

00:49:12.741 --> 00:49:16.036
I would say-- I would
say, definitely,

00:49:16.036 --> 00:49:19.998
I love Ousmane Sembéne.

00:49:19.998 --> 00:49:24.127
I mean, he's a prolific
African storyteller.

00:49:24.127 --> 00:49:28.632
And I think it was interesting
because at the time that he was

00:49:28.632 --> 00:49:32.386
directing, a lot of
African countries

00:49:32.386 --> 00:49:36.848
were achieving independence.

00:49:36.848 --> 00:49:39.184
And they were
navigating what it meant

00:49:39.184 --> 00:49:42.270
to be a post-colonial
state, and what it meant

00:49:42.270 --> 00:49:44.231
to craft your own identity.

00:49:44.231 --> 00:49:49.069
So a lot of his films speak
to that in one way or another.

00:49:49.069 --> 00:49:52.531
And I find his
projects, his stories,

00:49:52.531 --> 00:49:55.826
especially revisiting them now,
I find it very interesting.

00:49:55.826 --> 00:49:59.913
Because I feel, at least,
specifically for Kenya, like,

00:49:59.913 --> 00:50:06.128
we are reimagining what
it means to be Kenyan

00:50:06.128 --> 00:50:12.801
and what it means to be a
country that's not really

00:50:12.801 --> 00:50:16.555
forged its own identity
after decades of colonialism.

00:50:16.555 --> 00:50:18.765
I think we're still grappling
with that as a country.

00:50:18.765 --> 00:50:21.810
So that's what-- I mean, his
work really resonates with me.

00:50:21.810 --> 00:50:24.396
And I'll just say because
he's a classical filmmaker.

00:50:24.396 --> 00:50:25.772
He's fantastic.

00:50:25.772 --> 00:50:28.567
And then there's this other
African filmmaker called,

00:50:28.567 --> 00:50:30.027
Djibril Diop.

00:50:30.027 --> 00:50:32.279
I can never say his
first name properly.

00:50:32.279 --> 00:50:37.117
But he did this amazing fiction
film called Touki Bouki.

00:50:37.117 --> 00:50:40.203
I would just urge
everyone to just watch it.

00:50:40.203 --> 00:50:47.669
Djibril Diop, who's related to
Mati Diop, who did Atlantic.

00:50:47.669 --> 00:50:51.173
I mean, his films were
very experimental.

00:50:51.173 --> 00:50:52.841
And I just found it interesting.

00:50:52.841 --> 00:50:56.720
I was like, a guy in the '70s
and '60s was experimenting,

00:50:56.720 --> 00:50:58.680
I mean, with this new form.

00:50:58.680 --> 00:51:00.265
That's so brave and fearless.

00:51:00.265 --> 00:51:01.016
And yeah.

00:51:01.016 --> 00:51:04.644
I think those are the
people I really look up to.

00:51:04.644 --> 00:51:12.194
And I mean, internationally,
you know, you Roger,

00:51:12.194 --> 00:51:14.571
as a filmmaker, I mean,
you're a great pioneer.

00:51:14.571 --> 00:51:20.744
I would say Ava, with the work
she's doing, with a great crew.

00:51:20.744 --> 00:51:26.041
I mean, there's just so
many inspiring filmmakers.

00:51:26.041 --> 00:51:28.835
I mean, the list is quite long.

00:51:28.835 --> 00:51:30.545
But those are the ones
that come to mind.

00:51:30.545 --> 00:51:31.588
Yeah.

00:51:31.588 --> 00:51:38.303
Well, I would say that you,
Toni, are an inspiration

00:51:38.303 --> 00:51:43.558
not only being the
youngest producer invited

00:51:43.558 --> 00:51:48.146
to the Academy, youngest
African woman producer invited

00:51:48.146 --> 00:51:53.902
to the Academy, but as governor
of the doc branch, when

00:51:53.902 --> 00:51:56.905
we looked around the
continent for someone

00:51:56.905 --> 00:52:03.328
who could really speak to the
new movement on the continent

00:52:03.328 --> 00:52:08.416
in nonfiction filmmaking, and
who is doing incredible work,

00:52:08.416 --> 00:52:13.130
and just could represent
the community, you know,

00:52:13.130 --> 00:52:16.800
we didn't have to look
any further than you.

00:52:16.800 --> 00:52:20.262
So I thank you for
everything that you've

00:52:20.262 --> 00:52:26.268
done for Kenya and East Africa
and the continent and all

00:52:26.268 --> 00:52:31.356
the filmmakers that you inspire
and all that we're going

00:52:31.356 --> 00:52:33.525
to hear from you in the future.

00:52:33.525 --> 00:52:34.651
You have so much.

00:52:34.651 --> 00:52:38.405
I mean, you're just bubbling
up with so much energy.

00:52:38.405 --> 00:52:42.159
And the work that we are
going to receive from you

00:52:42.159 --> 00:52:43.910
is going to be so incredible.

00:52:43.910 --> 00:52:47.581
So thank you so much
for what you do.

00:52:47.581 --> 00:52:50.792
And thank you for being
part of this conversation.

00:52:50.792 --> 00:52:54.212
And yeah.

00:52:54.212 --> 00:52:57.174
It's so great to talk to
you, so great to talk to you.

00:52:57.174 --> 00:52:57.674
Same here.

00:52:57.674 --> 00:52:58.175
Yeah.

00:52:58.175 --> 00:52:59.176
I'm so excited.

00:52:59.176 --> 00:52:59.801
Thank you.

00:52:59.801 --> 00:53:01.511
It was amazing.

00:53:01.511 --> 00:53:02.262
Great.

00:53:02.262 --> 00:53:03.513
And there's more of us coming.

00:53:03.513 --> 00:53:04.598
There's more of us coming.

00:53:04.598 --> 00:53:06.391
It's not just me.

00:53:06.391 --> 00:53:08.935
There is.

00:53:08.935 --> 00:53:09.477
Get ready.

00:53:11.980 --> 00:53:16.526
Well, I want to thank the
Academy Visiting Artists Series

00:53:16.526 --> 00:53:19.863
and Toni for being here with us.

00:53:19.863 --> 00:53:21.907
Toni, this was a pleasure.

00:53:21.907 --> 00:53:23.491
And thank you so much.

00:53:24.451 --> 00:53:25.619
Thank you so much, Roger.

00:53:25.619 --> 00:53:27.829
It was really amazing
talking to you.

